No, don't just raise the pool size — the log shows `idle=0` while `waiting` climbs steadily (7→28 over 2.5 min), meaning connections are being held, not that 20 is too few. The bug is in `db.js:11-15`: if `fn(client)` throws, `client.release()` on line 14 never runs, so that client leaks out of the pool permanently. Under any error rate, leaked clients accumulate until the pool is exhausted, exactly matching the log pattern.

Fix `withClient` to always release:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Raising `max` would only delay the exhaustion, not fix the leak.
